    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The word imperialism took its present sense in English in the late 1870s, but the extension of power over foreign nations is as old as history; its most intense modern phase, the Age of Imperialism, is commonly said to have ended in 1914.</li><li>Its advocates justified it as a civilizing mission backed by theories of race, while imperial rule brought atrocities such as those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herero-and-nama-genocide">Herero and Namaqua genocide</a> and the ethnic cleansing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a>.</li><li>Hobson and Lenin explained imperialism as a product of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, movements in the colonies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism</a> among them, won independence and ended most of the European empires.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>’s victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1905 shook the world: never before had an Asian nation defeated a European power, and it became an example for Asian countries in the fight against the Western powers for decolonization.</p></aside>
    </section>

