    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1830</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> seizes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, beginning its second colonial empire</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1870s</span><span class="rf-text">The term imperialism is applied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, introduced in its present sense by opponents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-disraeli">Benjamin Disraeli</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1902</span><span class="rf-text">John A. Hobson publishes Imperialism: A Study, arguing that empire drained money needed at home</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1908</span><span class="rf-text">After scandal upon scandal over atrocities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> is forced to take it over from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1916</span><span class="rf-text">Lenin’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism-the-highest-stage-of-capitalism">Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism</a> portrays imperialism as monopoly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> on a world scale</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1947</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> becomes independent, with partition, as decolonization movements arise across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1962</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> grants <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> independence, ending most of its second colonial empire</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1978</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-said">Edward Said</a>’s Orientalism describes Western knowledge of the East as a justification of empire</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-disraeli"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benjamin-disraeli.jpg" alt="Benjamin Disraeli" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-disraeli">Benjamin Disraeli</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benjamin_Disraeli,_Earl_of_Beaconsfield_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cornelius Jabez Hughes, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The word imperialism comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> imperium, “to command” or “to rule”. Coined in the 19th century for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>’s attempts to gain political support by invasion, it was introduced into English in its present sense in the late 1870s by opponents of the imperial policies of the British Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-disraeli">Benjamin Disraeli</a>, and supporters such as Joseph Chamberlain quickly took it up. By the 1880s it had a positive connotation in the West, and by the end of the century it described the conduct of empires at all times and places. For some it meant idealism and philanthropy; others saw political self-interest or capitalist greed.</p><p>Empire itself is far older: imperialism has been present since the beginning of history. In ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, Tianxia placed the imperial court at the centre of a divinely ordered world that reached out to the fringe “barbarians”, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> drew local elites into its culture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>’s ambitions showed in the Tudor conquest of Ireland from the 1530s and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, established in 1599. The Age of Imperialism, whose beginning is dated between 1760 and 1870 and whose end commonly to 1914, saw European nations, helped by industrialization, railways, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> and, by the 1880s, the machine gun, intensify colonizing and annexation; in the late 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> joined them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Imperialism is the maintaining and extending of power over foreign nations by hard power, military and economic, and by soft power, diplomatic and cultural. Its advocates justified it in several ways. Social Darwinism and theories of race gave it a supposedly rational basis: in 1883 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-ferry">Jules Ferry</a> declared that “Superior races have a right, because they have a duty. They have the duty to civilize the inferior races.” The civilizing mission and the White Man’s Burden set “primitive” peoples against “advanced” ones. Environmental determinism held that temperate climates produced hard-working, moral people and tropical ones laziness and degeneracy, so that tropical peoples needed European guidance.</p><p>The political geographers Friedrich Ratzel, who held expansion necessary for a state’s survival, and Halford Mackinder supported empire, and British settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> was arguably premised on terra nullius, as its settlers considered the land unused by its original inhabitants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-said">Edward Said</a> argued in Orientalism (1978) that Western scholarship built an imagined East, irrational and backward, as the ideological justification of Western imperialism, and the blank spaces of 19th-century maps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> gave the powers an incentive to fill them. John Gallagher and Ronald Robinson held that British leaders sought empire “informally if possible and formally if necessary”, through trade and capital rather than rule.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/opium-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/opium-wars.jpg" alt="Opium Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/opium-wars">Opium Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Destroying_Chinese_war_junks,_by_E._Duncan_(1843).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Edward Duncan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> built the largest empire the world has seen. Its “First” empire, based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercantilism">mercantilism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, was checked by the loss of the American colonies in 1776; it turned to India, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, Asia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the Pacific, and after 1815 enjoyed a century of almost unchallenged dominance, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-britannica">Pax Britannica</a>, which was still an almost uninterrupted series of colonial wars, from the Anglo-Burmese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opium-wars">Opium Wars</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">Scramble for Africa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in 1830 and after 1850 rebuilt a worldwide empire in North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>, which at its height in the 1920s covered over 11,500,000 km², with 110 million people on the eve of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> began a colonial empire in Africa and the South Pacific in 1883–84, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>’s empire began with the purchase of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assab-bay">Assab Bay</a> in 1869 by an Italian navigation company. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, under the motto “Enrich the state, strengthen the military”, annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a> in 1879, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> after the First Sino-Japanese War, annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1910, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1931 and by 1942 had conquered much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and the Pacific. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, once opposed to imperialism, took territory from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, where war broke out in 1899. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> expanded across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, and critics argue that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, though it declared itself anti-imperialist, carried on the old Russian tradition of expansion and control.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Imperial rule rested on force and extraction. The British exploited the political weakness of the Mughal state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the economist Utsa Patnaik estimates the wealth transferred out of India between 1765 and 1938 at $45 trillion. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, run by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> as a private <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> enterprise, scandal after scandal over atrocities forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> to take it over in 1908. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> committed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herero-and-nama-genocide">Herero and Namaqua genocide</a>, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of the 20th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>’s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> brought the slaughter of indigenous peoples, among them the Daur, Koryaks, Itelmens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansi">Mansi</a> and Chukchi, and its war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> their ethnic cleansing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> institutionalized compulsory labour and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in its colonies, and the treatment of indigenous peoples in the westward expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> has been called “internal colonialism”.</p><p>Empires also built roads and infrastructure and brought new technologies, and Stephen Howe credits the great empires with stability, security and legal order for their subjects. Their profits were uneven: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> gained from India but not from most of its empire, and Germany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> got little from theirs. The colonized resisted: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> cost the United States much more than expected because of military action against rebels, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-war-of-independence">Irish War of Independence</a> of 1919–1921 created the Irish Free State, and after 1945 anti-colonial movements fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah.jpg" alt="Kwame Nkrumah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kwame_Nkrumah_on_a_Soviet_stamp_(80th_anniversary_of_his_birth)_1989_CPA_6101.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">USSR Post, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Criticism grew with empire itself. John A. Hobson’s Imperialism: A Study (1902) argued that financing overseas empires drained money needed at home and held down domestic wages, and that social reform could cure imperialism by removing its economic foundation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> built on Hobson in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism-the-highest-stage-of-capitalism">Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism</a> (1916), portraying imperialism as monopoly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> on a world scale that made imperialist wars inevitable; the treatise remained a standard textbook until the Soviet collapse. Joseph Schumpeter and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannah-arendt">Hannah Arendt</a> defined imperialism as expansion for the sake of expansion. In 1965 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> called neocolonialism “the last stage of imperialism”, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-rodney">Walter Rodney</a>, in How <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> Underdeveloped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> (1972), described a world divided into exploiters and exploited.</p><p>Others disputed these theories. By the 1970s David Fieldhouse, David Landes and Oron Hale argued that modern imperialism was chiefly a political product of national mass hysteria rather than of capitalists, and Fieldhouse wrote that no empire had any definable function, economic or otherwise. Kenneth Waltz traced it to great power itself: “Weakness invites control; strength tempts one to exercise it.” Scholars also disagree on how imperialism differs from colonialism. In 1898 Americans founded the Anti-Imperialist League against the annexation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, and the former general Smedley Butler denounced American foreign policy as a “racket”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh.jpg" alt="Ho Chi Minh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ho_Chi_Minh_-_1946_Portrait_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Anti-imperialism gained wide currency after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, as political movements in the colonies of the European powers promoted national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism</a>, which sought to unite Africans divided by imperial rule and whose conferences, among them the Pan African Conference of 1900, gave Africans a voice in ending colonial rule, helped end colonialism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> and other young radicals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> had formed the French Communist Party in 1920. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> became independent, with partition, in 1947; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> fought and lost a war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in the 1950s and granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> independence in 1962; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>’s independence in 1960 ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>’s colonial experiment, and most of Portugal’s African colonies became independent in the 1970s.</p><p>The word lives on, often as a pejorative in international propaganda. Since the 2010s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> under Vladimir Putin has been described as neo-imperialist, for the 2008 invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, the 2014 annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and the 2022 invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and it is accused of neo-colonialism in Africa. Scholars have widened the term to cultural imperialism, the soft power that changes how a people sees its own life, and to a world system reaching back centuries, in some accounts to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>. The rhetoric of racial superiority still leaves traces, as in the prizing of “whiteness” throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Imperialism is the maintaining and extending of power over foreign nations. Named in the 19th century, it drove the European empires, joined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, to conquer and rule much of the world, justified by a civilizing mission and theories of race and enforced with great violence. Critics from Hobson and Lenin to Said analysed it, and decolonization after 1945 ended most of its empires.</em></p>

